  "body": "[COEP's updated \"cross-origin resource policy check\" algorithm](https://mikewest.github.io/corpp/#abstract-opdef-cross-origin-resource-policy-check) includes the following steps:\r\n\r\n> 2. Set embedder policy to \"none\" if both of the following statements are\r\n>    true:\r\n>    - request's client's embedder policy is \"none\".\r\n>    - request's reserved client is not null, and its embedder policy is\r\n>      \"none\".\r\n\r\n[The reserved client is only set for navigation requests and top-level requests for Dedicated/Shared Worker scripts.](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-request-reserved-client) The worker case is irrelevant because the mode of those requests is always \"same-origin,\" so this algorithm does not apply. This step appears to concern navigation requests specifically.\r\n\r\nDuring [\"process a navigate fetch\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#process-a-navigate-fetch), the request's reserved client is an environment. However, COEP does not introduce an \"embedder policy\" for environments (though it does introduce one for environment settings objects, along with Document objects and WorkerGlobalScope objects).\r\n\r\nAlso, \"process a navigate fetch\" does not seem to set the embedder policy for the request's reserved client. I believe this indicates another oversight. By my reading, either the embedder policy of the reserved client should be set in \"process a navigate fetch\", or the value of the embedder policy should not be referenced in the statement cited above.\r\n\r\n@zcorpan @mikewest @annevk",
